# Part II — Requirements as the Foundation

## Chapter 5 — The Requirement Completeness Problem

**Chapter objective:** Investigate the most demanding prerequisite of Integral Development: an adequate specification of the entire intended product before autonomous realization begins.

### 5.1 — What Does Complete Mean?

Develops a precise definition of requirement completeness relative to a defined product scope and operational environment. Distinguishes a specification that supports acceptance decisions from an impossible description of every potential future circumstance.

### 5.2 — Functional and Behavioral Completeness

Examines the specification of functions, operational states, interactions, exceptional conditions, and failure behavior. Discusses how scenarios, state machines, use cases, and formal behavioral models can reveal missing requirements.

### 5.3 — Nonfunctional Requirements and System Constraints

Explores performance, safety, reliability, maintainability, security, manufacturability, cost, and environmental constraints. Explains why these requirements must be incorporated into global engineering decisions rather than treated as secondary optimization objectives.

### 5.4 — Detecting Missing, Ambiguous, and Contradictory Requirements

Introduces AI-supported completeness analysis, requirement dependency graphs, consistency checking, and structured questioning. Explores the possibility of agents identifying missing specification elements and obtaining explicit decisions before realization starts.

### 5.5 — Establishing the Requirements Baseline

Defines the conditions under which a specification can be approved for an Integral Development Transaction. Introduces baseline identifiers, requirement provenance, controlled assumptions, uncertainty registers, and acceptance contracts.

## Chapter 6 — From Human Intent to Executable Requirements

**Chapter objective:** Explain how stakeholder intent can be transformed into a structured, machine-interpretable engineering specification.

### 6.1 — Natural Language as the Starting Point

Explores how stakeholders typically express product needs through descriptions, examples, existing systems, regulations, and informal expectations. Examines ambiguity, incompleteness, and the role of domain-specific terminology.

### 6.2 — Requirement Extraction and Semantic Structuring

Describes agent-based extraction, normalization, classification, and linking of requirements. Introduces semantic relationships between functions, interfaces, constraints, operational conditions, and verification criteria.

### 6.3 — Formal Models and Executable Contracts

Discusses formal languages, design-by-contract, state machines, temporal logic, constraints, and model-based systems engineering. Explains how machine-readable statements can support both design generation and automated checking.

### 6.4 — Building the Executable Product Specification

Proposes the architecture of the EPS as a collection of interconnected models covering requirements, interfaces, behavior, constraints, assumptions, and acceptance criteria. The EPS becomes the authoritative reference for agentic realization.

### 6.5 — Validating the Specification Before Execution

Describes automated consistency checks, simulation of specified behaviors, coverage analysis, and stakeholder review of generated scenarios. Establishes a specification qualification gate intended to prevent avoidable errors from propagating into the autonomous execution process.

## Chapter 7 — Consistency, Dependencies, and Global Optimization

**Chapter objective:** Establish requirements engineering as a system-wide constraint and optimization problem.

### 7.1 — Modeling the Product as a Constraint System

Represents requirements as interconnected constraints over potential architectures, component parameters, behaviors, and resource allocations. Explains feasible solution spaces and how constraints restrict possible products.

### 7.2 — Requirement Interdependencies

Explores dependencies among functional, performance, safety, cost, and physical requirements. Introduces graph-based representations and techniques for detecting hidden coupling between apparently independent decisions.

### 7.3 — Detecting and Resolving Conflicts

Discusses contradictory requirements, infeasible specifications, and incompatible objectives. Distinguishes conflicts agents can resolve through design optimization from conflicts requiring explicit stakeholder decisions.

### 7.4 — Multi-Objective Product Optimization

Introduces Pareto optimality, weighted objectives, hard and soft constraints, and trade-off analysis. Explores automated comparison of candidate system architectures against multiple competing requirements.

### 7.5 — Global Coherence as a Continuous Invariant

Develops the principle that every accepted engineering artifact and decision must remain consistent with the current authoritative system model. Describes continuous consistency checking throughout realization and the handling of deviations.

