# Part IV — The Integral Development Process

## Chapter 13 — Initiating an Integral Development Transaction

**Chapter objective:** Define the operational procedure and mandatory entry criteria for an autonomous realization process.

### 13.1 — The Transaction as the Fundamental Unit

Defines an IDT as a bounded process governed by a specific specification, resource allocation, execution policy, and completion criteria.

### 13.2 — Specification Qualification and Feasibility

Describes completeness assessment, conflict detection, resource estimation, technical feasibility analysis, and validation of acceptance criteria before execution.

### 13.3 — Establishing the Execution Environment

Covers agent selection, tool configuration, repositories, test environments, simulation assets, access rights, and computing resources.

### 13.4 — Governance, Authorization, and Risk Boundaries

Introduces decision rights, approval requirements, security controls, safety restrictions, spending limits, and escalation policies.

### 13.5 — The Transaction Initialization Protocol

Proposes a reproducible protocol that records the requirements baseline, orchestration configuration, engineering environment, verification plan, and authorization to execute.

## Chapter 14 — Autonomous Realization and Convergence

**Chapter objective:** Describe the dynamic engineering process executed internally during an Integral Development Transaction.

### 14.1 — Generating the Realization Plan

Explains how orchestration agents derive engineering tasks, dependencies, responsibilities, resource estimates, and validation activities from the EPS.

### 14.2 — Parallel Design and Implementation

Describes concurrent development of subsystems and supporting engineering artifacts while enforcing architectural and interface constraints.

### 14.3 — Continuous Verification and Feedback

Explains how simulation, analysis, testing, and integration provide evidence that informs subsequent autonomous actions.

### 14.4 — Convergence Strategies

Introduces defect prioritization, constraint satisfaction, solution-space exploration, and optimization strategies. Explains how the system determines whether changes improve overall product compliance rather than merely addressing local symptoms.

### 14.5 — Termination, Recovery, and Escalation

Defines successful convergence, infeasibility detection, resource exhaustion, deadlock, and failure to meet confidence thresholds. Examines controlled recovery strategies and the circumstances requiring human intervention.

## Chapter 15 — Completion, Acceptance, and Delivery

**Chapter objective:** Define what it means for an autonomously developed product to be complete and how that completion is demonstrated.

### 15.1 — Defining Product Completion

Distinguishes implementation completion, integration completion, verification completion, and acceptance. Establishes objective criteria for declaring a successful transaction.

### 15.2 — Requirements Coverage and Compliance Evidence

Describes comprehensive traceability from requirements through design and verification results. Introduces coverage metrics and evidence packages.

### 15.3 — System-Level Validation and Qualification

Explores end-to-end behavior, operating conditions, fault scenarios, physical qualification, and user-oriented validation.

### 15.4 — Reproducible Delivery Packages

Defines the components of a complete deliverable: product artifacts, source files, design models, build environments, documentation, test results, known limitations, and traceability records.

### 15.5 — Acceptance and Transition to Operation

Examines stakeholder acceptance, deployment, manufacturing release, operational monitoring, maintenance handover, and future change control. Clarifies that a single integral delivery does not imply a product will never require modification.

