# Part V — Evidence, Economics, and Boundaries

## Chapter 16 — Measuring Integral Development

**Chapter objective:** Establish an empirical framework for determining whether Integral Development offers advantages over established methodologies.

### 16.1 — What Constitutes Development Performance?

Defines relevant dimensions of engineering performance: elapsed time, labor, computational resources, quality, risk, maintainability, and product fitness.

### 16.2 — Productivity and Time-to-Completion Metrics

Explores lead time, engineering effort, process overhead, parallel efficiency, verification cost, and total resource consumption.

### 16.3 — Quality, Completeness, and Defect Metrics

Defines requirements coverage, defect density, defect severity, escaped defects, architectural consistency, and acceptance performance.

### 16.4 — Experimental Comparison with Conventional Development

Proposes comparative studies involving equivalent requirements, comparable product scopes, controlled engineering resources, and independent acceptance evaluation.

### 16.5 — Interpreting Results and Avoiding Misleading Conclusions

Discusses selection bias, task complexity, unequal tool capabilities, differing levels of specification quality, and the challenge of generalizing from successful demonstrations.

## Chapter 17 — Economics and Scalability of Autonomous Engineering

**Chapter objective:** Analyze the conditions under which Integral Development becomes economically advantageous.

### 17.1 — The Cost Structure of Conventional Engineering

Examines labor, coordination, integration, verification, rework, infrastructure, and project-management costs.

### 17.2 — The Cost Structure of Integral Development

Introduces model inference costs, computational resources, software tools, infrastructure, supervision, formalization effort, and autonomous verification expenses.

### 17.3 — Parallelization and Coordination Overhead

Discusses limits on speed improvements from increasing agent count. Examines task dependencies, coordination complexity, resource contention, and diminishing returns.

### 17.4 — Requirements Quality as an Economic Factor

Explores the relationship between initial specification effort and downstream implementation cost. Investigates whether more comprehensive requirements engineering offsets the reduction in human execution effort.

### 17.5 — Economic Break-Even and Scalability Models

Develops theoretical models for identifying product classes, complexity levels, and organizational conditions in which Integral Development may outperform alternative methodologies.

## Chapter 18 — Failure Modes, Safety, and Trust

**Chapter objective:** Analyze the risks introduced by autonomous development and establish principles for reliable and accountable execution.

### 18.1 — Incomplete or Incorrect Requirements

Examines how omissions and errors in the initial specification can propagate through an otherwise correctly executed development transaction.

### 18.2 — Agent Errors and Correlated Failures

Discusses systematic reasoning errors, hallucinated assumptions, model limitations, shared biases, and failure propagation between agents.

### 18.3 — Verification Gaps and False Confidence

Explores inadequate test coverage, incorrect simulation models, circular validation, fabricated evidence, and failures outside the modeled operational domain.

### 18.4 — Security, Safety, and Regulatory Constraints

Analyzes secure execution, supply-chain risks, safety engineering, human oversight, independent assessment, and sector-specific regulatory obligations.

### 18.5 — Trustworthy Autonomous Engineering

Proposes a trust framework combining transparency, reproducibility, evidence provenance, independent verification, explicit responsibility, and controlled intervention mechanisms.

