# Part VI — Demonstration and Future Perspectives

## Chapter 19 — A Complete Integral Development Case Study

**Chapter objective:** Demonstrate the concepts developed throughout the book in a reproducible engineering example.

A suitable case study should be sufficiently complex to expose genuine system-level dependencies, but small enough that the complete development and verification process can be documented.

### 19.1 — Selecting and Defining the Reference Product

Introduces a representative multidisciplinary product, such as a networked measurement instrument combining electronics, embedded software, communication, user interaction, and mechanical packaging.

Defines product boundaries, operating context, constraints, and objective acceptance criteria.

### 19.2 — Creating the Complete Product Specification

Documents the transformation of stakeholder expectations into an EPS. Includes requirements completeness assessment, behavioral descriptions, interface constraints, and verification criteria.

### 19.3 — Configuring and Executing the Agentic Workflow

Describes the agent organization, orchestration strategy, connected engineering tools, execution environment, and internal activity records.

Traces architecture exploration, implementation, simulation, verification, and autonomous repair.

### 19.4 — Examining the Final Product and Verification Evidence

Presents the resulting design, implementation artifacts, integration results, validation evidence, and unresolved limitations.

Evaluates whether the delivered product satisfies the initial specification and whether the development process meets the definition of Integral Development.

### 19.5 — Comparative Evaluation and Lessons Learned

Compares the integral realization process with a conventional approach under defined experimental conditions.

Reports productivity, quality, resource usage, engineering effort, intervention frequency, and observed failure modes. Uses results to identify the strengths and limitations of the proposed methodology.

## Chapter 20 — Toward Autonomous Product Engineering

**Chapter objective:** Evaluate the broader implications of Integral Development and outline the scientific and technological advances necessary for adoption.

### 20.1 — The Changing Role of the Engineer

Explores the transition from direct product implementation toward requirements formulation, engineering intent, system constraints, validation design, and oversight of autonomous execution.

### 20.2 — Product Organizations Without Traditional Development Cycles

Considers how autonomous engineering might change project management, team structure, collaboration, release planning, and the separation between product ownership and implementation.

### 20.3 — Integral Development Beyond Software

Investigates applicability to electronics, mechanical engineering, multidisciplinary products, manufacturing systems, and other domains involving physical realization.

### 20.4 — Open Research Challenges

Identifies unresolved problems in requirement completeness, machine reasoning, autonomous architecture optimization, verification, multi-agent coordination, uncertainty management, and physical-world validation.

### 20.5 — From Development Methodology to Product Realization Paradigm

Revisits the central thesis and proposes a long-term vision of engineering systems that transform formalized product intent into complete, verified realizations with decreasing dependence on manual execution.

Concludes by identifying the empirical evidence necessary to establish Integral Development as a credible engineering discipline.

